<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="right"></a>
<u>15-SEP-2005 11:53 PM (AUS Time)</u>
Phillip Island is most famous for the Penguin Parade.  At dusk each night these tiny little dark-blue fairy penguins ride in on the waves of the ocean and waddle onto land to go back to their nests. The fairy penguins are the smallest penguins in the world.  They literally march up in groups to the beach and into their nests.  We would be on their domain, their turf watching what they do naturally.  We had to go check it out.
<p>
But like I said, that doesn't happen until dusk and we had all day.  We decided to go to the Koala Sanctuary instead because we were unable to spot koalas at all.  Finding out later that they are mostly night creatures explains why we didn't really see them in the day.  Koalas are only found in Australia and nowhere else.  They may look cute but don't mess with them, they have sharp claws.
<p>
Anyway, we were guaranteed koalas and the sanctuary didn't let us down. The sanctuary was set up amongst a swampy area and I didn't really see any gates or fences to keep the koalas in. They were free to do what they wanted and were just observed them naturally again.  
<p>
Looking up we spotted our first koala. They mix in well with the tree bark so they may be hard to find at first.  However, the one we saw was sleeping. The same story with the next one and so on and so forth.  M and I figured this would be the closest we would get to seeing a koala and since they are night creatures, they sleep during the day.  However, we entered an area of the sanctuary that contained a boardwalk which let you get onto their level high into the trees and this is where we spotted a big male koala. You can tell it's a male by the size of it's head. It should be much larger than it's body.  He was walking the wooden handrail of the boardwalk.  This little furry mate was a picture poser.  As soon as I whipped out the camera stop and waited for me to take my picture and then move on.
<p>
Feeling satisfied, we had time to kill so we drove to a nearby fur seal colony.  Being on the edge of Australia, we were in extremely windy territory.  The seals themselves were 2km away and you could only see them via a coin-operated binocular which was such a rip-off.  If you ever end up in Australia and this seal colony is on your list, don't bother. They are 2 km away, not visible with the human eye and even with the binoculars which lasted 2 minutes for $2, they looked like tiny brown dots.  However, if you want to just see the ocean crashing onto the rocks or the birdlife which has actually dug out holes on the ground to protect themselves from the fierce wind, check out this place.  I was looking for a better view of the seals so I was greatly disappointed.
<p>
With still more time to kill, we drove around to Rhyll and back.  M had been disappointed that she didn't see a single kangaroo while in Australia and our time was running out.  I was fortunate to see one on the first day and that was mainly it.  And just as we were talking about that, one popped out of the bush and hopped across the road and M was satisfied. Unfortunately, it was too fast for us to get a picture of it.
<p>
With enough time killed, we decided to head to the Penguin Parade Observation area.  Again, it was quite cold here so we had bundled up from head to toe and sat on the concrete stairs waiting for dusk and those little fairy penguins to show up.
<p>
About 6:35 PM, those little birds started to show up and they were much smaller than I had expected.  Maybe only 8 inches tall and they would struggle to get onto land and waddle their way up to their nests. Often then would come in groups and march up the beach.  This would last for 50 min but we were already tired and ready to go home so we left after 30 min.  Sorry I wasn't allowed to take any video or photos as they were not allowed, but I can tell you they were cute little fellows and made little squeaky noises as they moved through the bush to their nest.  
<p>
It was time to head back to Melbourne and return the rental car.  But first we needed a hostel to stay in and I remember seeing one the day before called the Friendly Backpacker.  It was right in the heart of Melbourne and I picked it because it would be easy access to things we wanted to see tomorrow.
<p>
We needed to unload the car and unforunately, there was no parking spot nearby so I had illegally parked hoping that we could get the car unloaded quickly and get out of there before a cop shows up and gives us a ticket.  I had told M to watch the car as I unload it, but for some reason, she came in and left the car alone.
<p>
I was about to have my second run-in with the authorities. As I came out to the car, sure enough there was a police officer there writing up a ticket.  He had a little PDA with him that had a printing device. As I ran out to plead to him that I was just unloading the car and would move it, he wouldn't stop processing the ticket.  However, he told me that since I came out at exactly the right time before he had hit the print button, the ticket didn't print out and I was free to go and that I was extremely lucky! Whew.  
<p>
I quickly moved the car even though it had one bag left to unload. I didn't want to chance it and with no parking, I just took the rentar car out to the Melbourne airport which unfortunately is quite a ways outside of the city if you avoid the tolls like I did.  
<p>
Having finally returned the car, I was down to my last $25 AUD and thank goodness I had it too.  After dropping off the car, I had to take a bus back to the city and it costs $24 roundtrip. I bought roundtrip because I needed the bus ride back to the airport to fly out the next day.  They bus driver only took cash so I was left with just $1 to my name.
<p>
I finally got back to the hostel and ended my day by watching a movie called "Mimic" on their big projection screen.  I was way too wired to go back to sleep easily.  
